1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:04,500
150 million dollar C-17 cargo planes.

2
00:00:05,500 --> 00:00:08,200
87 ton hovercraft Elkacs.

3
00:00:09,380 --> 00:00:13,260
A worldwide supply network answerable only to the Department of Defense.

4
00:00:14,200 --> 00:00:16,580
The military's awesome transport tools.

5
00:00:17,160 --> 00:00:20,240
They can speed men and materiel to any war zone on Earth.

6
00:00:20,960 --> 00:00:24,100
Now, military movers on Modern Marvels.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:36,480
The stage is set.

8
00:00:37,440 --> 00:00:38,760
The fighting ensues.

9
00:00:39,800 --> 00:00:44,420
No matter what war, no matter what battle, no matter what era,

10
00:00:45,180 --> 00:00:47,160
this will be the climax of the drama.

11
00:00:48,400 --> 00:00:50,060
But this is act three of the play.

12
00:00:51,280 --> 00:00:55,000
In order to wage and sustain any war, much work has to be done.

13
00:00:55,001 --> 00:01:00,740
Any battle actually begins long before the soldiers engage,

14
00:01:01,320 --> 00:01:04,600
when planners determined that it was possible to arrive at the battlefield.

15
00:01:08,040 --> 00:01:11,040
With aircraft, land vehicles, and ships,

16
00:01:12,160 --> 00:01:17,140
planners supply soldiers with weapons, ammunition, food, and medicine.

17
00:01:17,920 --> 00:01:21,280
And they maintain that supply using military movers,

18
00:01:22,220 --> 00:01:25,280
Transportation designed specifically to move military cargo.

19
00:01:27,020 --> 00:01:30,340
The infantry fights for what they call key terrain,

20
00:01:31,300 --> 00:01:34,020
but to understand these battles and where they occur,

21
00:01:34,500 --> 00:01:37,120
you have to understand how people get to the battlefield.

22
00:01:40,240 --> 00:01:43,480
Military movers can range from giant aircraft carriers,

23
00:01:43,800 --> 00:01:45,480
sometimes used to transport troops,

24
00:01:45,960 --> 00:01:49,700
to heavy-duty helicopters that lift supplies into the war zone.

25
00:01:51,280 --> 00:01:59,000
They have included trucks, barges, flat cars, boats, even Jeeps, and Humvees.

26
00:02:04,100 --> 00:02:07,360
This is the top of the line for today's military movers.

27
00:02:08,400 --> 00:02:14,220
A 174-foot-long, 270,000-pound C-17 cargo plane,

28
00:02:14,840 --> 00:02:20,940
capable of traveling at over 500 miles an hour, or over 3,200 miles on a single fueling.

29
00:02:21,280 --> 00:02:29,960
With four 42,000-pound turbofan engines, it is capable of 162,000 pounds of thrust.

30
00:02:30,400 --> 00:02:33,400
The C-17 has been a marvelous airplane. Its reliability is great.

31
00:02:33,960 --> 00:02:37,720
It allows us to move 18 pallets or any number of passengers into country

32
00:02:37,721 --> 00:02:41,560
to go into very short runways for takeoff and landings.

33
00:02:42,040 --> 00:02:46,040
And when we surgically went into Afghanistan and took out the runway,

34
00:02:46,041 --> 00:02:48,520
we then had to come and operate out of those same runways.

35
00:02:48,521 --> 00:02:56,120
The hold on this megaplane is capable of transporting Jeeps, Humvees, even tanks.

36
00:02:57,800 --> 00:03:08,420
It can also be reconfigured to accommodate 102 paratroops or 144 regular troops or 48 litters for medical evacuations.

37
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:14,000
The purpose of this plane is basically anything the Air Force wants it to do.

38
00:03:14,400 --> 00:03:17,880
It'll get things to the front line as soon as possible.

39
00:03:18,520 --> 00:03:23,940
The C-17 has some spectacular features that make it a favorite of logistics planners.

40
00:03:24,840 --> 00:03:27,260
One is its ability to refuel in midair.

41
00:03:28,100 --> 00:03:30,840
We have a regular C-17, which holds 180,000 pounds.

42
00:03:30,841 --> 00:03:36,900
We have an extended range, which I believe holds 220,000 pounds of fuel and gets us pretty far.

43
00:03:37,280 --> 00:03:40,920
And they have an aerial refueling receptacle on the top of the airplane.

44
00:03:41,600 --> 00:03:44,360
And we can stay in the air as long as they need us to.

45
00:03:44,361 --> 00:03:50,820
One other thing that makes this plane a little bit unique is we can fly with night vision

46
00:03:50,821 --> 00:03:55,860
goggles and so we can go into dimly lit or runways without any light.

47
00:03:56,360 --> 00:03:59,120
It also helps us to scan for threats in a combat environment.

48
00:04:00,220 --> 00:04:03,940
It's actually one of the easier planes to fly because it is all glass and it does a

49
00:04:03,941 --> 00:04:04,641
lot for you.

50
00:04:04,840 --> 00:04:08,740
We have autopilot and auto throttle so you can hook those up and you'll do everything

51
00:04:08,741 --> 00:04:09,441
for you.

52
00:04:09,442 --> 00:04:10,600
It's really a lot of fun to fly.

53
00:04:12,220 --> 00:04:14,340
The turbofan engines are a hybrid.

54
00:04:14,360 --> 00:04:17,060
of old piston-driven propellers and jets.

55
00:04:19,880 --> 00:04:23,140
The C-17 is the culmination of nearly a century

56
00:04:23,141 --> 00:04:25,480
of military aviation research and development.

57
00:04:26,580 --> 00:04:29,580
And it is the most recent of the many C-designated

58
00:04:29,581 --> 00:04:33,160
or cargo aircraft military movers in the Air Force arsenal.

59
00:04:35,220 --> 00:04:36,900
Before the 20th century,

60
00:04:37,660 --> 00:04:40,060
transportation consisted of land or sea travel.

61
00:04:40,940 --> 00:04:42,260
But within that century,

62
00:04:42,900 --> 00:04:47,180
air travel blossomed into one of the most effective and efficient logistical tools.

63
00:04:48,820 --> 00:04:52,840
Cargo aircraft really began with the more sophisticated twin-engine propeller planes

64
00:04:52,841 --> 00:04:58,040
developed in the late 1930s, specifically the C-47 and C-46.

65
00:04:58,580 --> 00:05:02,500
It's interesting to look at the quantum leap and evolution that takes place

66
00:05:02,501 --> 00:05:06,400
between World War I and World War II, where you jump up to a level of technology

67
00:05:06,401 --> 00:05:09,900
where you have multi-engine, piston-driven aircraft

68
00:05:09,901 --> 00:05:14,260
capable of carrying 6,000 pounds of cargo in the case of the C-47,

69
00:05:14,660 --> 00:05:16,960
10,000 pounds of cargo in the case of the C-46.

70
00:05:18,660 --> 00:05:23,160
The C-46 commando was capable of carrying as many as 50 men

71
00:05:23,161 --> 00:05:25,000
and had two loading doors.

72
00:05:26,760 --> 00:05:31,780
The more familiar C-47 carried only 28 men and had one door,

73
00:05:32,320 --> 00:05:34,620
but served in a combat role towing gliders

74
00:05:34,621 --> 00:05:36,880
and dropping paratroopers into enemy territory.

75
00:05:40,900 --> 00:05:46,180
The post-war period saw a series of piston-driven airplanes like the C-119 boxcar,

76
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:51,400
supplanted by the turboprop workhorse C-130 Hercules,

77
00:05:51,840 --> 00:05:55,820
and ultimately, the jet-powered C-141 Starlifter.

78
00:05:56,700 --> 00:05:59,600
The biggest of them all was the massive C-5.

79
00:05:59,601 --> 00:06:04,920
at the time of its introduction in the late 1960s, the largest aircraft in the world.

80
00:06:06,240 --> 00:06:13,740
But a military mover 247 feet long and 65 feet high with a 270,000-pound payload

81
00:06:13,741 --> 00:06:21,840
and a maximum takeoff weight of 840,000 pounds needs its space and about 8,000 feet of runway.

82
00:06:22,960 --> 00:06:25,180
And this is where the C-17 comes in.

83
00:06:25,181 --> 00:06:30,780
The smaller, more agile C-17 would do what the lumbering C-5 could never do,

84
00:06:31,420 --> 00:06:33,740
and in places the C-5 could never go.

85
00:06:36,480 --> 00:06:39,000
The C-17 was conceived in 1980,

86
00:06:39,440 --> 00:06:43,300
when the Air Force requested a versatile cargo plane with a large load capacity,

87
00:06:43,940 --> 00:06:47,900
combined with the ability to land at unimproved airstrips under battle conditions.

88
00:06:49,620 --> 00:06:53,120
The C-17 is obviously smaller than the C-5 Galaxy.

89
00:06:53,121 --> 00:07:04,160
The C-17's overall size is closer to, say, a C-130 or C-141, but at the same time, it can carry more than a C-130 or a C-141.

90
00:07:04,640 --> 00:07:11,860
So by having smaller aircraft that costs less money overall to manufacture, we can have a larger fleet of C-17s.

91
00:07:11,861 --> 00:07:25,861
One of the C-17's most important features is that it is a big empty shell.

92
00:07:27,860 --> 00:07:30,860
Loadmaster Scott Wedzel is in charge of filling this one.

93
00:07:30,861 --> 00:07:34,860
The loadmaster is a jack of all trades.

94
00:07:34,861 --> 00:07:39,860
For cargo missions, the loadmaster plans the cargo, where it's going to be placed in the

95
00:07:39,861 --> 00:07:43,060
to determine how much the aircraft weighs.

96
00:07:43,061 --> 00:07:45,220
We have to balance the aircraft for flight.

97
00:07:45,221 --> 00:07:50,120
When we carry passengers, the load master administers meals.

98
00:07:50,121 --> 00:07:53,700
We pass out blankets, pillows, comfort items for the passengers.

99
00:08:00,800 --> 00:08:04,080
Loading this 18 foot wide, 15 foot high compartment

100
00:08:04,081 --> 00:08:08,920
with 172,000 pounds of freight requires a computer, a crew,

101
00:08:08,921 --> 00:08:10,340
had a plan.

102
00:08:10,341 --> 00:08:13,640
Right now the aircraft is set up to download the rolling stock.

103
00:08:13,641 --> 00:08:15,860
Once the rolling stock is off the aircraft,

104
00:08:15,861 --> 00:08:17,420
we would need to reconfigure the floor

105
00:08:17,421 --> 00:08:19,200
to move the pallets out.

106
00:08:19,201 --> 00:08:21,380
To do that, first thing we're going to do

107
00:08:21,381 --> 00:08:23,740
is flip over all the rollers.

108
00:08:23,741 --> 00:08:26,100
We raise the guide rails.

109
00:08:26,101 --> 00:08:27,780
The aircraft would be open.

110
00:08:27,781 --> 00:08:31,220
We'd come over here to the side panel.

111
00:08:31,221 --> 00:08:33,800
These pallets are locked into the side rails.

112
00:08:33,801 --> 00:08:35,920
The locks are electrically controlled.

113
00:08:35,921 --> 00:08:37,640
They're going to retract into the floor,

114
00:08:37,641 --> 00:08:39,600
and that will release the pallet.

115
00:08:39,601 --> 00:08:41,340
Then all we would do is simply push the pallet

116
00:08:41,341 --> 00:08:42,260
off the aircraft.

117
00:08:46,800 --> 00:08:49,160
Specially designed hydraulic forklift cranes

118
00:08:49,161 --> 00:08:52,640
called K-loaders help load the C-17

119
00:08:52,641 --> 00:08:54,320
and can even be driven right on board

120
00:08:54,321 --> 00:08:55,640
through the back loading ramp.

121
00:08:57,200 --> 00:09:00,600
It reaches up to 225 inches up in the air.

122
00:09:00,601 --> 00:09:04,180
It can uphold 25,000 pounds of cargo

123
00:09:04,181 --> 00:09:06,000
or rolling stock at one time.

124
00:09:07,640 --> 00:09:12,860
The C-17 began assisting military operations in the Balkans in the early 90s,

125
00:09:13,300 --> 00:09:15,380
having just missed action during the Gulf War.

126
00:09:16,180 --> 00:09:19,880
Since then, it has proven itself in hundreds of humanitarian sorties,

127
00:09:20,500 --> 00:09:23,140
dropping 2.5 million individual rations

128
00:09:23,141 --> 00:09:25,900
during Operation Enduring Freedom in Afghanistan

129
00:09:25,901 --> 00:09:28,160
and Operation Iraqi Freedom.

130
00:09:30,040 --> 00:09:31,780
I believe it's the cream of the crop.

131
00:09:31,781 --> 00:09:34,700
I fly on them as well, and they've done things

132
00:09:34,701 --> 00:09:36,680
that I've never seen any other plane do before.

133
00:09:37,640 --> 00:09:42,477
It's got great maneuverability. Short, distant landing, takeoff.

134
00:09:42,478 --> 00:09:46,180
It can haul anything. It's great. Great airplane.

135
00:09:48,260 --> 00:09:52,480
A specially constructed flight simulator helps pilots get a feel for the C-17.

136
00:09:54,040 --> 00:09:57,060
But much of the actual flying is done with onboard computers.

137
00:09:57,660 --> 00:10:01,620
This is what we use to control the aircraft for turning and climbing.

138
00:10:02,320 --> 00:10:04,380
These are our throttles, and this gives us the power.

139
00:10:05,100 --> 00:10:06,420
We have our flaps right here.

140
00:10:07,140 --> 00:10:08,700
Down in here, this is our computer,

141
00:10:09,640 --> 00:10:10,440
corporate flight plan,

142
00:10:10,780 --> 00:10:13,060
and you put where you're going to fly to in the computer,

143
00:10:13,600 --> 00:10:14,880
and it'll come up on here.

144
00:10:15,220 --> 00:10:17,440
So you can hook it up to the autopilot,

145
00:10:17,560 --> 00:10:18,620
and it'll fly it for you.

146
00:10:20,560 --> 00:10:21,540
Not surprisingly,

147
00:10:22,080 --> 00:10:24,200
in addition to the 120 aircraft,

148
00:10:24,201 --> 00:10:26,320
which will be fully delivered by 2004,

149
00:10:27,160 --> 00:10:30,980
the military has requested another 60 C-17s.

150
00:10:30,981 --> 00:10:37,300
And until the next generation of aircraft flies from the drawing board into the war zone,

151
00:10:38,060 --> 00:10:43,920
the C-17 Globemaster III will remain the most flexible of all the airborne military movers.

152
00:10:45,400 --> 00:10:52,500
C-17 supplied 50% of the cargo and more than 4,000 passengers for the United Nations peacekeepers in Bosnia.

153
00:10:53,100 --> 00:10:55,900
Military movers will return on Modern Marvels.

154
00:11:00,980 --> 00:11:11,240
In June of 1944, a Louisiana shipbuilder helped break the back of the Axis powers when the Allies gained a logistical stronghold in occupied France.

155
00:11:14,680 --> 00:11:19,180
Andrew Jackson Higgins built the boats that brought victory to Europe in World War II.

156
00:11:20,520 --> 00:11:27,720
Eisenhower told historian Stephen Ambrose that Andrew Higgins was the man who won the war for us, which is an incredible statement.

157
00:11:27,721 --> 00:11:35,540
Now, Adolf Hitler also called Higgins the new Noah because of the boats that Higgins designed and developed.

158
00:11:37,720 --> 00:11:47,164
Hitler and his generals had spent years fortifying the occupied European coastline with obstacles that included coast artillery emplacements in what became known as

159
00:11:47,165 --> 00:11:48,200
the Atlantic Wall.

160
00:11:48,201 --> 00:11:57,040
Deepwater ports like Cherbourg and Le Havre or nearby Calais were the most likely invasion

161
00:11:57,041 --> 00:12:03,377
points and thus most heavily fortified, which is why the Allies didn't invade there.

162
00:12:03,378 --> 00:12:04,078
Instead,

163
00:12:04,079 --> 00:12:10,240
they knew that Andrew Higgins had designed a boat that made those ports unnecessary.

164
00:12:10,241 --> 00:12:15,960
All of Higgins' many boat designs had shallow drafts to float over hidden mines and a nearly

165
00:12:15,961 --> 00:12:18,520
flat bottom that enabled them to sail right onto the beach.

166
00:12:21,080 --> 00:12:26,020
Certain boats had a ramp-like door in the front that allowed fast unloading of troops,

167
00:12:26,300 --> 00:12:28,760
jeeps, tanks, even small bulldozers.

168
00:12:29,100 --> 00:12:35,540
Thanks to Higgins, whenever the Allies went to invade, they no longer had to sweep a harbor

169
00:12:35,541 --> 00:12:36,241
of mines.

170
00:12:36,600 --> 00:12:41,080
They no longer had to take an enemy-held port in order to land their assault troops.

171
00:12:41,680 --> 00:12:45,780
The Higgins vote was of absolute crucial importance to the war-winning effort in World

172
00:12:45,781 --> 00:12:51,700
war ii mainly because of the fact that it gave the allies an ability to transition troops in big

173
00:12:51,701 --> 00:12:57,860
ships in deep water across an open beach and to allow them to go into combat this is an ability

174
00:12:57,861 --> 00:13:06,000
that prior to that was largely unheard of higgins was an enterprising louisiana timber importer

175
00:13:06,001 --> 00:13:11,880
who realized he could make more money converting his timber into boats which he sold retail than

176
00:13:11,881 --> 00:13:13,152
he could selling wood.

177
00:13:13,153 --> 00:13:17,140
They started making boats for the trappers and the oilfield companies

178
00:13:17,141 --> 00:13:20,920
to allow them to go into the bayous, into the shallow waters that could navigate over

179
00:13:20,921 --> 00:13:26,540
sandbars, that could pull up on the side of a bank and retract without damaging the bow

180
00:13:26,541 --> 00:13:27,839
or the propeller.

181
00:13:27,840 --> 00:13:33,340
When Higgins saw that the Navy was trying, without much luck, to develop

182
00:13:33,341 --> 00:13:38,200
a military landing craft throughout the 1930s, he thought he could sell them his Higgins

183
00:13:38,201 --> 00:13:41,273
boat design. At first, they weren't interested.

184
00:13:41,274 --> 00:13:44,280
He was an Irishman who said he didn't wait for

185
00:13:44,281 --> 00:13:48,291
opportunity to knock. He'd kick the door down and drag the old harlot in.

186
00:13:48,292 --> 00:13:49,720
He told them that his boat

187
00:13:49,721 --> 00:13:53,994
was better. He told them in explicit words why their boats were inadequate.

188
00:13:53,995 --> 00:13:55,020
And he didn't mind

189
00:13:55,021 --> 00:13:59,788
telling the admirals face to face what he thought of it. The Navy disliked this.

190
00:13:59,789 --> 00:14:00,920
They saw him as the

191
00:14:00,921 --> 00:14:06,119
small, arrogant boat builder from the South. The Marine Corps saw him as a savior.

192
00:14:06,120 --> 00:14:06,880
The Marines,

193
00:14:06,881 --> 00:14:11,060
the only service practicing landing operations in the late 1930s,

194
00:14:11,560 --> 00:14:14,540
realized that the Higgins boats did what Higgins said they could do.

195
00:14:16,220 --> 00:14:19,080
And since the Marines were the ones that were going to be landing in them,

196
00:14:19,700 --> 00:14:21,200
they forced the Navy to test them.

197
00:14:22,800 --> 00:14:27,100
Sure enough, the boats could ride right up onto the beach without doing any damage.

198
00:14:28,220 --> 00:14:31,140
The skag underneath the boat was built out like a housing,

199
00:14:31,640 --> 00:14:35,900
protecting the propeller and digging a channel in the sand when the boat rammed the beach.

200
00:14:36,880 --> 00:14:39,820
This also made retraction easier.

201
00:14:42,080 --> 00:14:49,220
A special interest to the military was a feature adapted from a Japanese landing barge, a drop ramp front.

202
00:14:50,780 --> 00:14:56,440
You could load 36 armed men standing in the upright position with packs on their backs and rifles in their hands,

203
00:14:57,440 --> 00:15:01,820
run that thing up onto the beach, drop the ramp, and in a matter of seconds, literally a matter of seconds,

204
00:15:02,220 --> 00:15:06,460
they could run off of that landing craft in the upright position and deploy into combat.

205
00:15:06,880 --> 00:15:14,600
It was recognized soon thereafter that having that ability was very useful to use in a tactical situation

206
00:15:14,601 --> 00:15:18,500
where a landing was being conducted under opposed circumstances.

207
00:15:19,240 --> 00:15:21,640
In other words, landing troops under fire.

208
00:15:23,660 --> 00:15:29,760
Higgins designed what became the Navy's LCP, or Landing Craft Personnel, for landing soldiers.

209
00:15:30,720 --> 00:15:35,700
LCVP, or Landing Craft Vehicle Personnel, for landing soldiers and equipment,

210
00:15:36,160 --> 00:15:37,760
such as jeeps and small bulldozers.

211
00:15:38,500 --> 00:15:43,180
And LCM, or Landing Craft Mechanized, for unloading armored vehicles.

212
00:15:44,920 --> 00:15:48,420
In fact, Higgins designed more than 60 different landing craft.

213
00:15:50,500 --> 00:15:54,700
American mass production ensured there would be enough of them to overwhelm the enemy.

214
00:15:55,660 --> 00:16:02,780
The United States was able to produce and equip practically all of our allies with landing craft throughout the war.

215
00:16:03,220 --> 00:16:07,120
Higgins Industries in New Orleans alone made 20,000 landing craft.

216
00:16:07,760 --> 00:16:14,320
This was quite an order for a small boat builder, because the boats were built almost entirely out of wood, except for the metal ramp.

217
00:16:15,480 --> 00:16:17,980
Metal would have been too expensive and complicated to use.

218
00:16:17,981 --> 00:16:23,940
Higgins managed to acquire the last three shiploads of Philippine mahogany

219
00:16:23,941 --> 00:16:26,080
before the Japanese invaded the islands

220
00:16:26,081 --> 00:16:30,760
Mahogany was used because it was a very strong hardwood

221
00:16:30,761 --> 00:16:36,300
With the orders stacking up, Higgins created a larger factory

222
00:16:36,301 --> 00:16:40,660
Meanwhile, he began assembling boats in the streets of New Orleans

223
00:16:40,661 --> 00:16:42,420
until the facilities were ready

224
00:16:42,421 --> 00:16:46,200
With all the work that Higgins needed to accomplish for the war effort

225
00:16:46,201 --> 00:16:50,240
he was able to employ a huge percentage of New Orleans working population.

226
00:16:51,520 --> 00:16:57,500
Having 20,000 people, he knew he needed every able-bodied person he could get and race didn't

227
00:16:57,501 --> 00:16:58,201
matter to him.

228
00:16:58,202 --> 00:17:00,900
He hired people out of beauty parlors, he hired people who were chauffeurs,

229
00:17:01,460 --> 00:17:06,467
which didn't really sit well probably with the local community businesses.

230
00:17:06,468 --> 00:17:07,820
He was paying higher

231
00:17:07,821 --> 00:17:12,813
wages because he needed the people and the employees loved tickets.

